WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.090
Hello, the internet. I'm Gav.

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.720
Welcome to this Slow Mo Guy.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.160
A lot of the videos I try and make

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.570
are around familiar subjects.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.140
Something you may seen a few times

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.020
or even used but then you
get to see it in slow mo.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.870
This video is nothing like that.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.460
It's about a very specific
piece of lab equipment

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.560
called an ultrasonic
homogenizer or a sonicator

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.850
as it's sometimes known.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.730
There's a bunch of applications for this

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.020
but one of the main ones

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.990
is you can use it to mix liquids

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.280
that wouldn't usually mix.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.860
Like oil and water, for example.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.360
And how it does it is
by vibrating this tip,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.360
this little metal tip tens of
thousands of times per second.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.800
This one will vibrate up
and down just slightly

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.330
about 30,000 times in a single second.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.390
And we're gonna see what
that looks like in slow mo.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.090
Obviously, because it's
30,000 times a second,

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.410
if I use the regular Phantom Flex4K,

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.510
we're limited to 1,000
frames a second in 4K,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.020
which means it'll be
moving 30 times up and down

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.620
in a single frame.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.570
That's no good.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:06.770
We do need to go faster.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.390
We ideally need to be
at frame rates higher

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.967
than 30,000 frames a second.

00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.040
And a lot of the time,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.260
in an actual science lab,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.700
you'll see these in their
own little enclosures

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.550
with a door but this isn't as fancy.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.950
This is the cheapest one I could find

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:20.910
and it didn't come with any of that.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.130
So we're just gonna stick
it in this little clamp

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.870
that I may or may not
have stolen from the set

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:27.840
of Planet Slow Mo.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.270
As you know, on this channel,

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.490
for 90% of the videos,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.270
we use the Phantom Flex4K,

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.330
which is a very lovely 4K image

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.653
at 1,000 frames a second.

00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:37.090
But when we need to go faster than that,

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.310
we typically use one
of the V series camera.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.470
It's the 2511 supplied by Destine,

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:42.560
which is a science camera.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.310
Much lower resolution but
much faster frame rate.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.740
Okay, let's start with just
a simple glass of water

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.950
just to see its effects on the liquid.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.000
Got our macro lens on

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.630
so we'll get really close to the tip.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.950
I'm gonna start full resolution,

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.223
which will give us 28,500 frames a second.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.963
Oof, so loud.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:09.920
Wow, this is interesting.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.900
Yes, so what we're seeing
here is cavitation bubbles.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.320
Basically the sonicator is moving so fast

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.010
and so frequently that it's just tearing

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.700
these little voids in the
water where a bubble forms

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.050
but there's not actually air in there.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.660
It's just a gap in the water.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.870
And then the forces opposing that cause it

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:27.950
to collapse in

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.080
but because the sonicator
is moving so fast,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.930
they're constantly opening and closing

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.050
to create these shock waves
all throughout the ripple

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.620
and every time one of
these bubbles implodes,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.997
it also creates heat.

00:02:37.997 --> 00:02:39.340
And it seems to be causing the liquid

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.250
to flow straight down from the middle

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.270
and then around the sides.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.030
So I think if we do this
again with oil on the top,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.160
we'll see the oil get sent down the probe.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.930
So here I'm adding some vegetable oil.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.540
Quite a large serving.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.100
But this should mix them together

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.623
into a sort of emulsion.

00:03:15.073 --> 00:03:18.130
Ah yes, so you can see
the flow it creates.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.680
All of the bashed liquid
goes straight down the middle

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.006
of the tip.

00:03:23.006 --> 00:03:27.470
So all the oil is then
drawn in to fill that void.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.710
So it actually gets mixed down the probe

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.738
and then spreads out to the sides.

00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:33.660
You can see here that the oil

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.010
is sending the tip out of focus

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.650
but you can see here,
it's smashing the oil

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:37.763
into tiny particles.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:51.910
Okay, we've seen what it
does submerged in liquid.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.620
Now I just wanna see what
it does to a single drop,

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.200
balanced on the top.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.423
I'll film this about
40,000 frames a second.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.730
All right, instantly vaporized.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.290
Just smashed into about a million pieces.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:08.010
It even got the stuff that
I'd spilled further down

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.733
and it collected in the
base of the sonicator head.

00:04:19.844 --> 00:04:23.760
Whoa.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.180
It looks so big.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.703
It scales it up so much.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.930
This is like a scaled-down version

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.130
of what the scarecrow was
trying to do in "Batman Begins"

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.850
where he's trying to use
the ultrasonic weapon

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.610
to vaporize all the city's water.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.960
Just absolutely aerosolizes all of it.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.510
Just makes it completely breathable.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.480
I can tell because of the
way the camera can't decide

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.120
what color to make them,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.700
some of these droplets are
smaller than a single pixel

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.533
on the sensor.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.820
And it looks as though as the sonicator

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.980
is moving up and down,

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.150
the downward movement is
drawing the air into it

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.430
so that as the droplets
are coming back around,

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.350
they're getting sucked back on

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.233
and just punched even smaller.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.340
All right, let's just
do one obscenely fast

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.740
so we can actually see the proper movement

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.300
of the sonicator head.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.480
We won't see much in terms of detail here

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.630
because we're not even 400
pixels wide in this resolution

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:34.850
but we do have 171,000 frames a second

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.670
and I need so much light for this shot

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.350
that even just slightly offsetting

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:41.010
my back light looks
completely pitch black.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.750
So the only way I can see anything

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:46.480
is by shining the light
directly in the lens hole.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.400
The exposure time is so low

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.440
that the light refracting
through the droplet

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.690
isn't even bright enough.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.190
Okay, I'm talking to
you know from the edit

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.420
as we analyze this clip.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.899
I wanna find out how long it takes

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:02.490
for the sonicator to do
a full up-down cycle.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:03.810
So I'm just gonna speed
it on a little bit here

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:04.643
so we can see it.

00:06:04.643 --> 00:06:08.610
If we do the maths and
divide one second by 30,000,

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:13.300
it gives us 0.00003333 of a second.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.640
So I if pull up the absolute time here,

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.330
we should be able to look at time directly

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.260
for the answer instead
of using the calculator.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.330
Firstly, pay attention to how slowly

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.040
the second is ticking by.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.560
Even on milliseconds,

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.900
ticking by painfully slowly at this speed.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.050
So we're mainly looking
in the microsecond area.

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:31.980
I'm now gonna slow the foot down

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.810
in post to about 10%,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:34.987
giving us the equivalent

00:06:34.987 --> 00:06:38.520
of almost two million
seconds a frame playback.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.450
And then we'll pick an
arbitrary up and down cycle

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.763
and just pay close attention to the time.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.140
Yeah, so that's very close.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.137
That's a difference, that's .000035

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:01.640
as opposed to what we calculated
there with the .000033.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:04.540
So slightly slower than the 30 kilohertz

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.290
but definitely in that ball park.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.060
Interestingly, there are some forms

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.430
of humidifiers you can buy,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.050
which use ultrasonic tech like this

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.680
to smash water up and get it in the air.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.280
It's one way of humidifying a room.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.800
Doing it one drop at a time
though, not recommended.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.050
It will take you much, much longer

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:25.923
to increase your relative humidity.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.600
I now want to learn whether
an apple enjoys being vibrated

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.593
at a very fine point at 30 kilohertz.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.573
Burning it, oops.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.770
Sonicated the Phantom there a little bit.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:53.940
It seems to be causing such
tiny concentrated pockets

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:58.880
of heat that the apple is
just immediately burning

00:07:58.880 --> 00:07:59.970
at that point.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.610
It's crazy that if my frame
rate is below the frequency

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.720
of the sonicator, it
just looks like a blurb.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.250
Because it's moving back
and forth so many times,

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:10.550
the picture of it is being taken

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:11.630
at a different spot each time.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:15.150
So it just looks like a blurry slow thing

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.103
when it's actually a
supremely fast punchy thing.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.153
This is bizarre.

00:08:20.153 --> 00:08:22.470
I'm not sure whether this is
creating any cavitation bubbles

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.700
in the liquid of the apple

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:28.286
or whether it's just shear friction.

00:08:28.286 --> 00:08:29.119
We should try this on something

00:08:29.119 --> 00:08:30.743
that doesn't have any moisture in it.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.480
It still seems to be able to
cook its way through wood.

00:08:37.085 --> 00:08:37.918
Whoa.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:44.140
There's no way I'm not
wrecking this thing.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.020
This is just the friction.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:57.300
This could potentially be
used as a fire starter.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.300
I bet you could get am ember

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.450
if it's actually causing
the wood to smoke.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.090
This thing's a fire hazard.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:09.050
Will it go through the
cloth of my lab coat?

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.980
Okay, three, two, one.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:11.813
Oh, immediately.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:17.727
Absolutely no resistance at all.

00:09:17.727 --> 00:09:18.943
I'm gonna try an old T-shirt.

00:09:21.178 --> 00:09:23.653
If I can find where this is.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.010
All right, I blocked your few of that,

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:28.310
sorry about that.

00:09:29.541 --> 00:09:31.171
You'll get to see it on slow mo there.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.720
Yeah, then when one of
the holes gets big enough,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.283
the whole thing just smashes through.

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:54.863
Well, there we have it.

00:09:54.863 --> 00:09:56.730
Some very interesting and
rather bizarre footage

00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:00.088
of the intended use and
also some unintended use

00:10:00.088 --> 00:10:02.540
of a ultrasonic homogenizer.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:03.373
I may have wrecked it.

00:10:03.373 --> 00:10:06.170
It is looking slightly
charred at the end now.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.030
I mean, I don't really
plan on using it again.

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.220
I'll stick it in a box
just in case though.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.800
If you can think of
any other lab equipment

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:14.920
that would be very interesting

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.540
to see how it work in slow mo,

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:17.770
let me know in the comments.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:19.890
I might be able to get my hands on some

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.480
and do another one of these.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.940
Hopefully you enjoyed that video.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.510
If you wanna be alerted to new slow mos,

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.630
make sure you subscribe
and hit the old bell

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:28.880
and give it a like if you liked it.

